@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.876 = private unnamed_addr constant [739 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for cases where files are opened (e.g., using the builtin `open()` function)\0Awithout using a context manager.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf a file is opened without a context manager, it is not guaranteed that\0Athe file will be closed (e.g., if an exception is raised), which can cause\0Aresource leaks. The rule detects a wide array of IO calls where context managers\0Acould be used, such as `open`, `pathlib.Path(...).open()`, `tempfile.TemporaryFile()`\0Aor`tarfile.TarFile(...).gzopen()`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afile = open(\22foo.txt\22)\0A...\0Afile.close()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Awith open(\22foo.txt\22) as file:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `open`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#open)\0A", align 1
