@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.930 = private unnamed_addr constant [690 x i8] c"## What it does\0AThis is not a regular diagnostic; instead, it's raised when a file cannot be read\0Afrom disk.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAn `IOError` indicates an error in the development setup. For example, the user may\0Anot have permissions to read a given file, or the filesystem may contain a broken\0Asymlink.\0A\0A## Example\0AOn Linux or macOS:\0A```shell\0A$ echo 'print(\22hello world!\22)' > a.py\0A$ chmod 000 a.py\0A$ ruff a.py\0Aa.py:1:1: E902 Permission denied (os error 13)\0AFound 1 error.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [UNIX Permissions introduction](https://mason.gmu.edu/~montecin/UNIXpermiss.htm)\0A- [Command Line Basics: Symbolic Links](https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/workflow-symbolic-links)\0A", align 1
