@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1009 = private unnamed_addr constant [1151 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for repeated equality comparisons that can be rewritten as a membership\0Atest.\0A\0AThis rule will try to determine if the values are hashable\0Aand the fix will use a `set` if they are. If unable to determine, the fix\0Awill use a `tuple` and suggest the use of a `set`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ATo check if a variable is equal to one of many values, it is common to\0Awrite a series of equality comparisons (e.g.,\0A`foo == \22bar\22 or foo == \22baz\22`).\0A\0AInstead, prefer to combine the values into a collection and use the `in`\0Aoperator to check for membership, which is more performant and succinct.\0AIf the items are hashable, use a `set` for efficiency; otherwise, use a\0A`tuple`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afoo == \22bar\22 or foo == \22baz\22 or foo == \22qux\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo in {\22bar\22, \22baz\22, \22qux\22}\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Comparisons](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#comparisons)\0A- [Python documentation: Membership test operations](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#membership-test-operations)\0A- [Python documentation: `set`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#set)\0A", align 1
