@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1042 = private unnamed_addr constant [700 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `set.remove` that can be replaced with `set.discard`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf an element should be removed from a set if it is present, it is more\0Asuccinct and idiomatic to use `discard`.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule is prone to false negatives due to type inference limitations,\0Aas it will only detect sets that are instantiated as literals or annotated\0Awith a type annotation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anums = {123, 456}\0A\0Aif 123 in nums:\0A    nums.remove(123)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anums = {123, 456}\0A\0Anums.discard(123)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `set.discard()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html?highlight=list#frozenset.discard)\0A", align 1
