@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.1052 = private unnamed_addr constant [1429 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `asyncio.create_task` and `asyncio.ensure_future` calls\0Athat do not store a reference to the returned result.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APer the `asyncio` documentation, the event loop only retains a weak\0Areference to tasks. If the task returned by `asyncio.create_task` and\0A`asyncio.ensure_future` is not stored in a variable, or a collection,\0Aor otherwise referenced, it may be garbage collected at any time. This\0Acan lead to unexpected and inconsistent behavior, as your tasks may or\0Amay not run to completion.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport asyncio\0A\0Afor i in range(10):\0A    # This creates a weak reference to the task, which may be garbage\0A    # collected at any time.\0A    asyncio.create_task(some_coro(param=i))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport asyncio\0A\0Abackground_tasks = set()\0A\0Afor i in range(10):\0A    task = asyncio.create_task(some_coro(param=i))\0A\0A    # Add task to the set. This creates a strong reference.\0A    background_tasks.add(task)\0A\0A    # To prevent keeping references to finished tasks forever,\0A    # make each task remove its own reference from the set after\0A    # completion:\0A    task.add_done_callback(background_tasks.discard)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [_The Heisenbug lurking in your async code_](https://textual.textualize.io/blog/2023/02/11/the-heisenbug-lurking-in-your-async-code/)\0A- [The Python Standard Library](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html#asyncio.create_task)\0A", align 1
